neutrino-dirac-mass-term-3f409763.interpretation:
The parameter $m_D$ represents the mass that neutrinos would acquire via the standard Higgs mechanism, in the same way as quarks and charged leptons. Its existence requires the postulation of right-handed (sterile) neutrinos, $
u_R$, that couple to the Standard Model Higgs boson. In the context of the Type-I seesaw mechanism, $m_D$ is a crucial mixing term in the neutrino mass matrix. The magnitude of $m_D$ is often hypothesized to be on the order of the electroweak scale (e.g., similar to other fermion masses), which contrasts sharply with the observed tiny active neutrino mass $m_{
u}$. This discrepancy is elegantly resolved by the seesaw formula, $m_{
u} \approx m_D^2 / M_R$, where $M_R$ is a very large Majorana mass for the right-handed neutrino.
